Your interpretation is plausible, but the figures are not directly comparable until the denominator and exclusions are confirmed.

- **Vendor figure: 99.1%** may be calculated only on parcels that produced a completed read-and-route decision, excluding no-reads, sensor faults, jams, diverted-to-manual items, re-inductions, or operator overrides.
- **Your figure: 94.3%** appears to use total inducted parcels as the denominator and treats handheld overrides and physical rejects as failures or manual interventions.
- The gap is approximately **4.8 percentage points**, which is consistent with excluded exception cases accounting for roughly that share of total induction volume, though the exact relationship depends on how each metric handles duplicate scans and re-inductions.

Use this reconciliation:

\[
\text{Overall accuracy} =
\frac{\text{correct automated routes}}
{\text{all unique inducted parcels}}
\]

Separate the following categories:

1. Correct automated routes  
2. Incorrect automated routes  
3. No-reads  
4. Sensor or system faults  
5. Manual interventions and handheld overrides  
6. Physical rejects  
7. Re-inductions or duplicate records  
8. Parcels excluded by the vendor methodology  

Request the vendor’s report definition, specifically:

- numerator and denominator;
- treatment of no-reads and exceptions;
- whether manual overrides count as failures;
- whether physical rejects are included;
- whether the rate is calculated per scan, parcel, pass, or completed routing decision;
- treatment of re-inducted parcels;
- time window and production conditions;
- confidence intervals or sample size.

The decisive test is to reconstruct the vendor metric from your logs using their stated exclusions. If the result approaches 99.1% only after removing incomplete or manually handled cases, then they reported **conditional completed-pass accuracy**, not end-to-end read-and-route accuracy. Your 94.3% figure may be the more operationally relevant measure, provided duplicate parcels and re-inductions are removed consistently.

Present it as a separate procedural fact, not as a justification for the telemetry or the injury:

> During the [date] shift, the conveyor belt stalled three times, as documented in the jam log. The incident report was recorded at [time] during the same shift. After the shift, I accessed and preserved the relevant telemetry and jam logs. Management subsequently issued a disciplinary notice characterizing that access as “unauthorized equipment tampering.” I did not alter equipment settings, bypass safeguards, modify records, or interfere with operations. My actions consisted of retrieving operational records for review. The disciplinary notice and the underlying access-policy basis should be evaluated separately from the accuracy data and incident chronology.

Include only facts you can document:

- date and exact times;
- the equipment identifier;
- number and duration of stalls;
- incident-report timestamp;
- when and how the logs were accessed;
- whether the system recorded any changes;
- the specific policy or authorization requirement cited;
- the discipline issued and its stated reason;
- whether you reported or preserved the data afterward.

Avoid statements such as “I did it to prove the vendor was wrong” or “the write-up was retaliation” unless you have evidence supporting those conclusions. Use “management characterized” rather than adopting the accusation as fact.

If the access was read-only, state that explicitly and attach system audit records showing no configuration or operational changes. If the policy is unclear, write:

> I have not identified a policy provision that defines read-only retrieval of telemetry as equipment tampering. Please identify the specific provision relied upon and distinguish data access from alteration of equipment or controls.

Do not access additional systems or records outside your authorization. Preserve the original discipline notice and request the applicable access-control and equipment-operation policy in writing.

