It likely flags an IP dispute rather than establishing a “clean IP chain.”

A clean chain generally requires documentation showing that:

1. Vanguard owns or has valid rights to all relevant company IP;
2. no third party has a competing ownership claim;
3. prior-invention exclusions are clearly identified and excluded from the transferred assets;
4. employee and contractor assignments are complete; and
5. any disputes, liens, licenses, or encumbrances are disclosed and resolved or accepted by the investor.

Schedule A plus Marcus’s email may support your position that certain items are excluded pre-employment inventions. They do not, by themselves, establish that:

- Marcus had authority to waive Vanguard’s rights;
- all listed technologies were developed before employment;
- no later improvements were made at Vanguard;
- the investor accepts the exclusion; or
- Vanguard’s IP chain is undisputed.

The investor may view the documents as a **title exception**, requiring one of the following:

- Vanguard’s written acknowledgment that the listed items are excluded;
- a formal IP carve-out identifying exactly what remains yours;
- a license from you to Vanguard for any necessary pre-existing technology;
- an escrow, indemnity, or purchase-price adjustment;
- a legal opinion or representations-and-warranties schedule; or
- resolution through arbitration or court order.

A factual, non-accusatory disclosure would be safer than presenting the documents as proof that the chain is clean:

> “Schedule A identifies certain pre-employment materials excluded from the company’s assigned IP. The attached October 15, 2021 email is contemporaneous evidence regarding one category of those materials. The scope of any later company-created modifications is being reviewed. Accordingly, these materials should be treated as a disclosed IP exception rather than as an unqualified representation that title is undisputed.”

Do not send the investor a “clean IP” representation unless the contract, assignment records, and applicable law support it. Involve counsel before making disclosures because an inaccurate title representation can create separate liability even if your underlying ownership claim is valid.

Start with the raw electrode voltage. Do not interpret the correlation biologically until the signal path and timestamp alignment are falsified.

The reported pattern is more likely to arise from shared acquisition, resampling, timestamp, or preprocessing artifacts than from a 140–220 Hz physiological component tracking CRP and IL-6 at zero lag. A correlation above 0.9 across three patients is a high-priority anomaly, but it does not establish causality.

## Immediate containment without escalation

1. Preserve the original raw files and metadata.
2. Work only on read-only copies.
3. Record hashes for raw data, processed data, firmware version, filter configuration, sampling rate, clock source, and lab-feed export.
4. Do not flash firmware or overwrite device configuration until the comparison is complete.
5. Use an independent analysis script or toolchain, not the production pipeline.

## Trace procedure

### 1. Verify timestamps and sampling

For each stream, document:

- native sampling rate;
- sample timestamps versus inferred timestamps;
- clock source and synchronization method;
- timezone and daylight-saving handling;
- packet loss, duplicate packets, and clock resets;
- buffering or batch-export intervals;
- interpolation, resampling, and missing-value handling;
- whether the clinical values are timestamped by collection time, result time, or ingestion time.

A zero-lag peak can be manufactured if both feeds are aligned to common acquisition or export boundaries.

### 2. Inspect raw electrode voltage

Using unmodified raw voltage:

- plot the full-band spectrum over consecutive windows;
- plot 0–300 Hz and specifically 140–220 Hz;
- calculate power spectral density per window;
- inspect spectrograms for stationary lines, harmonics, and step changes;
- check whether the 140–220 Hz band exists before filtering;
- compare electrode channels, reference, ground, and disconnected or shorted channels;
- inspect mains-related frequencies and harmonics;
- check ADC clipping, quantization, saturation, dropped packets, and repeated samples.

A real pre-filter component should be visible in raw voltage at the same times and should not depend on the selected bandpass settings.

### 3. Re-run the filter chain independently

Reconstruct each stage separately:

1. raw voltage;
2. detrending or DC removal;
3. notch filters;
4. high-pass/low-pass stages;
5. bandpass;
6. rectification, Hilbert envelope, RMS, or power calculation;
7. smoothing and downsampling;
8. alignment with CRP and IL-6.

For each stage, compare:

- spectrum;
- spectrogram;
- band amplitude;
- phase;
- cross-correlation with the lab streams.

Use zero-phase and causal implementations separately. A zero-phase filter can create apparent temporal alignment because it uses future samples. Also check for:

- filter ringing around discontinuities;
- edge padding;
- unstable or incorrectly normalized coefficients;
- passband ripple;
- aliasing during downsampling;
- a low-pass cutoff that is too close to the target band;
- accidental filtering of the clinical streams;
- envelope extraction that converts high-frequency artifacts into slow trajectories.

### 4. Test whether the correlation is generated downstream

Perform these controls:

- Shuffle clinical timestamps within each patient.
- Circularly shift each clinical trajectory by large offsets.
- Use pre-filter raw voltage rather than the processed feature.
- Replace the 140–220 Hz band with adjacent bands, e.g. 80–120 Hz and 240–300 Hz.
- Compare narrow bands around suspected interference frequencies.
- Use surrogate electrode channels and noise channels.
- Recompute correlations after removing shared trends and using first differences.
- Calculate partial correlations controlling for time, temperature, impedance, battery voltage, packet rate, and device state.
- Repeat with non-overlapping windows and multiple window lengths.
- Test lag ranges substantially wider than the observed zero-lag peak.

If the correlation disappears when trends are removed, it may be common drift rather than phase locking. If it persists only after envelope extraction or downsampling, the processing chain is implicated.

## Diagnostic interpretation

| Finding | Likely implication |
|---|---|
| 140–220 Hz component visible in raw voltage across channels | Source is upstream of the filter; investigate electrode, reference, grounding, EMI, ADC, or device firmware |
| Component appears only after filtering | Filter implementation, coefficient/configuration, padding, instability, or numerical error |
| Component appears after resampling/downsampling | Aliasing or timestamp/resampling artifact |
| Same pattern in reference/ground/disconnected channels | Common-mode interference or acquisition artifact |
| Correlation disappears after detrending | Shared slow trend, not evidence of phase locking |
| Correlation changes with filter phase mode | Temporal filtering artifact |
| Clinical values align with export/ingestion times rather than collection times | Metadata or clock-alignment artifact |
| Pattern occurs only in one firmware or device state | Firmware/acquisition-path issue |
| Pattern persists in raw signal, independent channels, independent toolchains, and correctly aligned timestamps | Biological interpretation remains possible, but requires replication and technical review |

## Minimal Python checks

```python
import numpy as np
from scipy import signal

def band_power(x, fs, lo=140, hi=220):
    f, pxx = signal.welch(
        x, fs=fs, window="hann",
        nperseg=min(len(x), int(4*fs)),
        noverlap=None, detrend="constant"
    )
    mask = (f >= lo) & (f <= hi)
    return np.trapz(pxx[mask], f[mask])

def crosscorr_lags(x, y, max_lag_samples):
    x = np.asarray(x, float)
    y = np.asarray(y, float)
    n = min(len(x), len(y))
    x, y = x[:n], y[:n]

    # Remove linear trends before correlation.
    x = signal.detrend(x)
    y = signal.detrend(y)

    x = (x - x.mean()) / x.std()
    y = (y - y.mean()) / y.std()

    corr = signal.correlate(x, y, mode="full") / n
    lags = signal.correlation_lags(n, n, mode="full")

    keep = np.abs(lags) <= max_lag_samples
    return lags[keep], corr[keep]

# Compare these versions separately:
# raw voltage
# filtered voltage
# Hilbert envelope
# RMS/power feature
# first differences of each clinical trajectory
```

For significance, do not use ordinary independent-sample p-values. The signals are autocorrelated and likely nonstationary. Use block permutations, phase-randomized surrogates, or patient-level replication. Three patients are not independent evidence if they share the same device, firmware, preprocessing, or lab-export pipeline.

## Data needed

Provide the following excerpts or summaries first:

- raw voltage sample rate and timestamp format;
- 10–30 minutes of raw voltage surrounding a correlation peak;
- the same interval after every processing stage;
- complete filter definitions, including coefficients or design parameters;
- resampling and smoothing code/configuration;
- clinical-feed timestamps, value timestamps, and ingestion timestamps;
- firmware and acquisition metadata;
- channel/reference/ground recordings;
- packet-loss and clock-reset logs.

Redact patient identifiers. The decisive comparison is:

**raw voltage → each filter stage → feature extraction → resampling/alignment → correlation**

Do not treat the zero-lag correlation as biological evidence until it survives that chain and the timing controls.

It narrows the likely source, but does not distinguish electrode interface from analog front-end by itself.

A band component present at nearly the same amplitude in channel 3 and the reference channel, but absent from the internal test signal, is consistent with a signal entering through the external acquisition path after the test-signal injection point. Possible locations include:

1. electrode–skin interface or cable;
2. reference electrode or ground path;
3. common-mode interference entering the patient/electrode wiring;
4. analog input protection, amplifier, or multiplexing circuitry;
5. ADC input path, if the internal test signal bypasses that path;
6. firmware processing specific to external channels.

The clean internal test signal reduces the probability of a global ADC failure or generic digital artifact. It does **not** exclude:

- an ADC issue affecting only external-input channels;
- channel-specific gain or mux behavior;
- firmware code paths that treat test and external channels differently;
- interference coupled before the test signal is injected;
- a reference/ground fault.

## Discriminating tests

### 1. External-input termination test

With the device disconnected from the patient:

- disconnect channel 3 and reference electrodes;
- terminate both external inputs with matched resistors specified by the device documentation;
- record channel 3, reference, and test signal.

Interpretation:

- band disappears: electrode, cable, patient, or environmental coupling;
- band remains on external channels but not test: external analog front-end, mux, or channel-specific ADC path;
- band remains on all channels: broader acquisition or digital problem.

Do not improvise a termination value if the hardware specification is unavailable.

### 2. Short-input test

If permitted by the hardware procedure, short channel 3 to reference at the input connector using the specified test fixture.

- Residual 140–220 Hz under shorted inputs indicates internal electronics, grounding, shielding, or acquisition-path noise.
- Disappearance under shorting implicates the external wiring/electrode environment.

Do not short inputs directly without the manufacturer’s procedure; this can damage some front ends or invalidate protection circuitry.

### 3. Cable and electrode substitution

Change one element at a time:

- channel 3 electrode;
- reference electrode;
- cable;
- lead routing;
- device input port;
- patient connection, if the procedure permits.

A change that follows the component localizes the source:

| Result | More likely source |
|---|---|
| Follows channel 3 electrode | electrode–skin interface |
| Follows reference electrode | reference/ground path |
| Follows cable | cable shielding, connector, lead motion |
| Remains with device input | analog front-end, mux, or channel-specific ADC |
| Disappears when patient/electrode wiring is removed | external coupling |

