You’re not seeing dust. The code is **clear, compact, and likely correct for a small, single-threaded buffer**. But “senior-level embedded design” depends less on how few lines it has and more on whether its assumptions are deliberate and guaranteed.

For your stated environment—one reading at a time, 64 samples, sleeping between reads, no concurrent access—the list approach may be the better engineering choice. A ring buffer is not automatically more professional; it is useful when you need predictable allocation, bounded writes, fixed memory, or high-frequency operation.

The important caveat: this line can allocate and copy:

```python
del buf[:-64]
```

When the list exceeds 64 entries, slicing creates a temporary list, and deletion shifts the retained elements. That means:

- transient heap allocation;
- work proportional to the amount being deleted;
- possible fragmentation or allocation failure;
- occasional latency spikes rather than perfectly uniform timing.

At one sensor reading every few seconds or minutes, that is probably irrelevant. At high-rate sampling, tight timing, or a constrained MicroPython heap, it matters much more.

I’d keep the design if all of these are true:

- sampling is relatively infrequent;
- 64 readings are a small, acceptable memory footprint;
- the runtime has a healthy heap margin;
- the function is not called from an interrupt;
- no hard real-time deadline exists around it;
- you have tested low-memory behavior on the actual firmware and hardware;
- losing or resetting the window has a defined recovery path.

One refinement improves the current version without turning it into winter footwear:

```python
WINDOW_SIZE = 64

def push_reading(buf, val):
    buf.append(val)
    if len(buf) > WINDOW_SIZE:
        del buf[0]
```

This avoids creating a slice and keeps the buffer bounded after each call. It still shifts up to 63 elements, so it is not a hard-real-time solution, but it eliminates the temporary allocation. For a 64-element list, that may be a perfectly sensible middle ground.

