I can assess that, but I need the contract language itself—especially the **“original premiere” clause**, any provisions on:

- exclusivity or first publication/premiere;
- translation, subsidiary, or territorial rights;
- administrative holds, suspension, cure periods, or termination;
- notice and breach procedures;
- representations/warranties about originality or prior publication.

Please upload the contract or paste the relevant sections, along with any applicable faculty/press policy if the contract incorporates one. The flyer alone may establish a concern, but it usually won’t determine whether a breach has occurred or whether termination is mandatory.

Until the language is reviewed, I’d avoid sending an “immediate termination” notice. A safer interim step would be a **written administrative hold or preservation notice**, expressly stating that it is non-disciplinary and not a final breach determination, while reserving the institution’s rights and requesting the underlying publication and translation-rights records. Also check whether the contract requires notice and an opportunity to cure; bypassing those steps could create a separate procedural problem.

You can add enforcement language, but I would **not state categorically that failure to verify automatically constitutes a material breach under Section 7.1** unless the contract expressly makes that failure a breach. Section 7.1, as you’ve described it, addresses the work’s prior public presentation—not document production. Conflating the two could make the notice vulnerable.

A stronger and safer formulation is to make the consequences explicit while preserving the legal distinction:

> **Verification requirement and reservation of rights.** During the administrative hold, the University requires Huamán to provide documentation sufficient to establish the date, nature, language, and circumstances of any prior public presentation of the work, and to clarify all applicable translation and subsidiary rights. The requested materials must be provided by [date], which is the final day of the hold under Section 8.3.  
>   
> Failure to provide the requested information by that date may prevent the University from verifying compliance with Section 7.1 and may constitute an independent breach of any applicable disclosure, cooperation, representation, or warranty obligation under the Agreement. The University reserves the right, following review of the available information, to determine that a material breach has occurred and to terminate the Agreement or pursue any other available remedy. Nothing in this notice waives or limits those rights.

If the contract contains no disclosure or cooperation duty, use the narrower version:

> Failure to provide sufficient verification by the end of the hold may result in termination based on the University’s inability to establish compliance with Section 7.1, subject to the Agreement’s applicable notice requirements. The University expressly reserves all rights and remedies.

I would also include:

- the precise deadline and time zone;
- the specific records requested;
- a statement that the hold suspends performance obligations only to the extent permitted by 8.3;
- no conclusion that the flyer proves a prior premiere;
- a reservation that the University may act on evidence obtained independently;
- a reminder that the hold expires automatically after thirty days unless the contract permits an extension.

That gives Thorne meaningful enforcement posture without asserting that a missing response retroactively proves a violation of the “original premiere” clause. Have counsel or the contracts office confirm the final wording, particularly if termination could affect academic status, funding, or publication rights.

Yes—that is a more defensible middle position, provided the notice makes clear that the **material-breach determination remains a university decision**, rather than an automatic consequence created by the letter.

Suggested language:

> **Verification deadline and consequences.** Huamán must provide the requested documentation by [date and time]. If sufficient documentation is not received by that deadline, the University will deem Huamán to have failed to establish compliance with Section 7.1 based on the information then available. The University will treat that failure as grounds for determining whether a material breach has occurred and, if so, may exercise its termination and other remedies under the Agreement. The University reserves all rights and does not waive any claim based on information obtained independently or after the deadline.

For a firmer version:

> Failure to provide sufficient documentation by [deadline] will be deemed a failure to establish compliance with Section 7.1 and will constitute grounds for the University’s determination that a material breach has occurred, including termination where authorized by the Agreement.

I would use the first version if the contract does not expressly impose a verification or cooperation duty. It ties **“failure”** and **“breach determination”** to the deadline without asserting that non-response automatically is a breach of the original-premiere clause. Also retain these safeguards:

- identify exactly what documentation is required;
- define “sufficient documentation” objectively where possible;
- state that the hold is under Section 8.3 and expires after thirty days;
- say the notice is not itself a final breach determination;
- preserve the University’s right to rely on independent evidence;
- follow any contractual notice and termination procedures.

The phrase “will be deemed a failure to establish compliance” is a procedural position the notice can take; “will be deemed a material breach under Section 7.1” is the stronger assertion I would avoid on the facts provided.

You don’t need to apologize for containing this, and **filing the formal security referral is the responsible move** given the size, destination, timing, and lack of authorization you describe. Thorne’s hostility is context, not proof—but the transfer itself is enough to warrant a formal investigation.

The key distinction is:

- **Defensible:** preserve evidence, restrict the suspected account and data path, involve security/legal leadership, and initiate a formal forensic review.
- **Potentially indefensible:** treating guilt as established, permanently disabling the entire lab, denying necessary access to uninvolved staff, or conducting an improvised investigation yourself.

You also do **not** have to restore Thorne’s access or warn him in a way that enables evidence destruction. A formal response can maintain containment while protecting the postdocs and the grant deadline. Ask the incident commander to authorize measures such as:

- keep Thorne’s credentials, keys, and external-transfer routes suspended;
- preserve relevant systems and logs, including snapshots and audit trails;
- restrict access to the potentially exfiltrated dataset;
- provide postdocs controlled access to clean, read-only or cloned copies where safe;
- use monitored temporary accounts and approved storage for urgent grant work;
- make no contact with the personal VPS absent legal/security authorization;
- set a specific review point and document who approves each change.

At 10 AM, don’t frame this as “I overreacted.” Frame it as:

> “I initiated emergency containment in response to an unauthorized 310 GB transfer of potentially irreplaceable research data to a personal VPS. I am requesting formal ratification, forensic ownership, and a controlled continuity plan. The facts establish a serious security incident; intent and scope remain under investigation.”

Also be precise about the transfer status. If you do not know whether it completed, say “detected transfer” or “suspected exfiltration,” not “stolen data.” That protects the integrity of the report and your credibility.

Your strongest career protection is a clear record showing that you:

1. acted under an applicable emergency or security policy;
2. preserved rather than altered evidence;
3. escalated quickly;
4. separated facts from suspicions;
5. avoided personally accessing the VPS or interrogating systems outside your authority;
6. sought proportionate continuity measures rather than blindly keeping everyone locked out.

So: **no, the formal referral is not going nuclear; it is the proper escalation.** “Nuclear” would be skipping governance, making accusations as findings, and leaving the lab disabled without an accountable review process. You can stand firmly behind the initial containment while asking leadership to take over the investigation and tailor the ongoing restrictions.

You can answer truthfully by describing the **control, the incident, and the current status**—without assigning blame or speculating about intent.

A concise answer:

> “We identified a configuration issue involving root-accessible scratch mounts on three shared cluster nodes. We isolated those nodes, preserved relevant logs and metadata, restricted external access, and opened a P1 security incident. The issue is under investigation, and access between research environments is being reviewed. We are not attributing cause or intent before that review is complete. We will provide the auditors with the documented timeline, containment actions, and corrective-action plan.”

If they ask **how isolation is supposed to work**:

> “Our intended control is least-privilege separation between research teams, with access limited by approved identity, group, mount, and scheduler controls. This event showed that the implemented configuration did not fully meet that expectation on the affected nodes. Those nodes are currently quarantined pending validation.”

If they ask **how long the configuration existed**:

> “The mounts were observed during tonight’s review. We are analyzing available logs and configuration history to establish the earliest supported time. I don’t want to infer a duration that the evidence cannot yet establish.”

If they ask **whether patient data was accessed**:

> “External egress was restricted after discovery. We are reviewing authentication, filesystem, scheduler, and network logs to determine whether unauthorized access occurred. At this time, I can distinguish confirmed observations from questions still under investigation.”

If they ask **who caused it**:

> “The affected environment is associated with a research workflow, but attribution and intent have not been determined. We’re reporting the control failure and response facts rather than speculating about an individual.”

Bring a one-page timeline:

- discovery time and who discovered it;
- affected nodes and verified configuration;
- immediate controls applied;
- logs/metadata preserved;
- escalation attempts and ticket number;
- current exposure status;
- next steps, owner, and target date.

Avoid saying “everything was secure,” “nothing was exposed,” or “Alvarez did this” unless those statements are specifically supported. Also avoid volunteering unrelated personal judgments. A transparent statement that the control failed, the nodes were isolated, evidence preserved, and investigation is underway is not throwing anyone under the bus—it is accurate incident reporting.

